WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.900
10,000 short period white dwarf binaries could soon reveal hidden exoplanets through spacetime ripples. This is Fascinating Frontiers Episode 250. After that, the oldest compact quasar pair, dwarf galaxies shedding metals, and green lasers built for landers, plus cosmic spotlight. Two new studies in Fizz.org coverage detail how the Lisa Observatory will detect gravitational waves from double white dwarf binaries and identify planets orbiting within those systems.

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:34.440
The first paper examining detection thresholds, while the second explores confirmation strategies

00:00:34.440 --> 00:00:36.140
with infrared observations.

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:41.360
A planet's gravitational tug imprints a Doppler modulation on the binary's gravitational

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:46.980
wave signal, appearing as sidebands at higher and lower frequencies, and the method works

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:51.660
best for planets with masses and orbital periods that produce those detectable sidebands.

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:57.200
NASA's James Webb Space Telescope could confirm candidates by detecting excess infrared

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:02.140
light from the planet, out to at least 500 light years, and the Milky Way contains

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:07.140
an estimated 10 million double-white dwarf systems with orbital periods under one hour.

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:14.200
Japanese astronomer Naoki Seto first proposed the concept in 2008, and Casey McGrath, a

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:19.440
postdoctoral researcher at the University of Maryland, Baltimore County, and NASA's Goddard

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:24.520
Space Flight Center, led the detection method paper and noted that an exoplanet's presence

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:27.240
becomes imprinted on the gravitational wave signal.

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:31.220
Scott Noble, a theoretical astrophysicist at NASA Goddard,

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:33.880
explained that the modulation adds sideband

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:37.000
similar to how radio carrier frequencies carry messages.

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:40.320
Mary Ann Limbach, an assistant research scientist

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:41.740
at the University of Michigan,

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:44.400
emphasized that joint detections with web

00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:46.480
will establish that gravitational signals

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:48.160
correspond to genuine planets.

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:51.340
White dwarfs are the Earth-sized remnants of stars

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:52.040
like the Sun.

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:58.880
No planets have yet been found around a binary white dwarf, and Lisa, led by ESA with NASA contributions,

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:03.620
including telescopes and hardware, is slated for launch in the mid-2030s.

00:02:04.686 --> 00:02:10.606
Napa Wawa Nakalua is the Hawaiian name given to the oldest pair of compact quasars ever observed,

00:02:10.926 --> 00:02:14.526
a system that formed only 900 million years after the Big Bang.

00:02:15.166 --> 00:02:21.126
No current theoretical model explains how two actively feeding black holes reach such high masses so early.

00:02:21.806 --> 00:02:26.386
Researchers discovered the system through observations that captured both objects in a single

00:02:26.386 --> 00:02:27.706
compact configuration.

00:02:28.266 --> 00:02:32.886
The masses sit inside a gap in understanding of how supermassive black holes assembled

00:02:32.886 --> 00:02:35.186
in the first billion years of cosmic time.

00:02:35.926 --> 00:02:40.606
Further observations of the pair could test whether mergers or rapid accretion drove their

00:02:40.606 --> 00:02:41.366
early growth.

00:02:42.157 --> 00:02:47.517
Dorf galaxies are the universe's most common residents, yet each one is only about one-tenth

00:02:47.517 --> 00:02:48.757
the width of the Milky Way.

00:02:48.757 --> 00:02:53.997
If the Milky Way stretches 100,000 light years across and holds hundreds of billions

00:02:53.997 --> 00:02:59.677
of stars, a typical dwarf galaxy spans roughly 10,000 light years and contains just a few

00:02:59.677 --> 00:03:07.897
billion stars. Hubble Space Telescope Imaging of NGC4449, released by NASA and processed

00:03:07.897 --> 00:03:13.157
by the Hubble Heritage Team, offers a clear example in the constellation Cain's Venetiti.

00:03:13.157 --> 00:03:18.997
The galaxy continues forming stars at a high rate despite its small size, yet its gas

00:03:18.997 --> 00:03:24.237
contains far less oxygen than chemical evolution models predict for its mass and star formation

00:03:24.237 --> 00:03:24.937
history.

00:03:25.457 --> 00:03:30.417
Those winds act like galactic bellows, sweeping oxygen and other heavy elements out of

00:03:30.417 --> 00:03:34.617
the shallow gravitational well of the dwarf and into intergalactic space.

00:03:35.117 --> 00:03:40.917
Because dwarfs vastly outnumber larger galaxies, their collective outflows may supply a surprising

00:03:40.917 --> 00:03:43.837
fraction of the heavy elements now found between galaxies.

00:03:44.497 --> 00:03:49.197
The same process helps seed future generations of stars and planets with the raw materials

00:03:49.197 --> 00:03:51.117
needed for rocky worlds in life.

00:03:51.857 --> 00:03:58.757
Observations of NGC4449 and similar systems reveal that the efficiency of this removal exceeds

00:03:58.757 --> 00:04:03.777
what current simulations assume, and the stellar winds and supernovae appear surprisingly

00:04:03.777 --> 00:04:06.837
effective at blowing away oxygen and other heavy elements.

00:04:06.837 --> 00:04:14.277
The image shows NGC 4449 as an irregular dwarf with active star-forming regions scattered

00:04:14.277 --> 00:04:15.297
across its disk.

00:04:15.917 --> 00:04:21.057
The outflows carry material beyond the galaxy's reach, enriching the surrounding intergalactic

00:04:21.057 --> 00:04:21.537
medium.

00:04:22.137 --> 00:04:26.797
Over cosmic time, this enrichment influences the chemical composition of gas that will

00:04:26.797 --> 00:04:28.517
later collapse into new galaxies.

00:04:29.157 --> 00:04:34.057
The open question is whether every dwarf galaxy behaves this way, or whether some retain

00:04:34.057 --> 00:04:39.117
more of their newly forged elements, quietly building up the chemical richness of their surroundings

00:04:39.117 --> 00:04:45.397
over billions of years. Spectra show the oxygen deficit directly, but nobody knows exactly how much

00:04:45.397 --> 00:04:51.197
material escapes versus how much falls back in recycled winds. Future observations with larger

00:04:51.197 --> 00:04:56.357
telescopes could map the extent of these outflows and measure the fraction of heavy elements that

00:04:56.357 --> 00:05:03.411
truly leave the galaxy forever. Tunable green diode lasers, in work described by Bioengineer.org,

00:05:03.411 --> 00:05:08.451
Now-enable, compact, ramen spectroscopy instruments suitable for space missions,

00:05:08.951 --> 00:05:15.531
providing the stable, narrow-land wood output needed to identify minerals and organic compounds on planetary surfaces.

00:05:16.011 --> 00:05:21.351
The design reduces size, weight, and power requirements compared with traditional laser sources.

00:05:21.931 --> 00:05:25.671
That cut-and-demand supports future landers and rovers that must perform

00:05:25.671 --> 00:05:28.611
in situ chemical analysis with limited resources.

00:05:28.611 --> 00:05:34.331
Raman spectroscopy works by shining laser light on a sample and measuring the scattered light

00:05:34.331 --> 00:05:40.071
to reveal molecular fingerprints. Green wavelengths improve sensitivity for certain minerals while

00:05:40.071 --> 00:05:46.205
remaining compact enough for spacecraft constraints. The United Arab Emirates in an Arabian business

